Once your container image is built, tested, and pushed to a registry, the final stage of CD (Continuous Delivery/Deployment) is releasing it to your production servers.

Here are the primary deployment strategies used in DevOps:

Recreate (Downtime Deployment):

Shuts down all old version containers first, then starts new version containers.

Trade-off: Simple, but causes downtime for users.

Rolling Update (Zero Downtime):

Gradually replaces instances of the old version with the new version one by one behind a load balancer.

Trade-off: Zero downtime, but both old and new versions run simultaneously during the rollout.

Blue-Green Deployment:

Runs two identical environments: Blue (current live version) and Green (new version).

Traffic is switched all at once via a router or load balancer to Green after validation.

Trade-off: Instant rollbacks (just switch traffic back), but requires double the infrastructure resources.

Canary Deployment:

Routes a tiny percentage of real user traffic (e.g., 5%) to the new version to monitor error rates and performance before rolling it out to 100% of users.
